{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The proposal explicitly states that 'reinstatement becomes preference-dependent rather than a purely structural derived property.' The problem context requires preserving 'reinstatement as a derived property.' If reinstatement is now conditional on the chosen preorder (an external input), it is no longer a structural derived property of the framework itself. The proposal concedes this breaks the guarantee, yet the problem requires the proposal to preserve reinstatement as a derived property.","counterexample":["Consider a framework with arguments A (root), B (attacks A), and C (attacks B). Under standard grounded semantics, C defeats B, so B cannot attack A, and A is reinstated: A is IN purely by the structure {A<-B<-C}. Under the preorder proposal, if the preorder ranks B as strictly preferred over C, then C is preferred-defeated by B. With no undefeated attacker of B, B is IN. Since B is now a most-preferred undefeated attacker of A, A is OUT. Reinstatement of A has failed solely because of the chosen preorder, not because the structure changed. Thus reinstatement is not a derived structural property; it depends on the external preorder, violating the problem's requirement that reinstatement remain a derived property."],"target":"3a53f5ca32e2a3e84e21fd159f2f9039f2e7f0a7f524191b2350760f0e2e1a9d"}]}